Run the test suite as caos jobs on a dev stack

The suite runs inside caos now. Every test is an entry (.caos-expr +
DEPS + worker.sh), the suite is a map-then fan-out, and the dev stack
is brought up inside a test-stack container with its own podman.

What changed, and why:

TESTS ARE ENTRIES. Each test carries .caos-expr, DEPS and worker.sh
(was cli.sh). The old tests/lib/run-test.sh harness is gone; running a
test is evaluating it. dev/run-test stages each test through eval,
launch and verdict as a chain of continuations.

MOST TESTS ARE WORKER TESTS. 35 of 46 tests were client tests that
cp -rL'd built images into a worktree; only 11 actually test the
client. Converting them (std/bash or dev/worker-test base instead of
dev/cli-test) took the salted suite from 29 client tests to 11.
dev/worker-test is a new image: git and tools, no client, no repo.

ONE NIX BUILD PER STACK BRING-UP. The flake produces caos-test-stack-
inputs (binaries + images), and dev/stack-up consumes it. Same path on
the host (caosd up) and inside the dev stack. build-builtins.sh is the
publisher in both.

EVERYTHING CARGO COMPILES LIVES UNDER rust/. Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock,
rust-toolchain.toml, crates/ all moved there. This lets a test declare
the workspace as a dependency (../../rust rust in DEPS) instead of
being handed the whole repo, and it retired a cleanCargoSource exclusion
that made editing test fixtures rebuild 176 deps. This is used by the
lint test

ONE flake.lock. Per-entry locks are gone; the root lock is DEPped and
the flake-builder places it. One lock to update, no lint to keep copies
honest. This required making deep-deps allow deps on files in addition
to dirs

THE DEV STACK RUNS ITS OWN PODMAN. Workers run with --network=host
inside the test-stack container, so they reach the server at 127.0.0.1.
No engine socket grant, no sibling containers on the host.

map-then --max-parallel BOUNDS THE FAN-OUT. A runner pool bounds
containers, but a test that has recorded a continuation and exited holds
no container while the work it waits for runs. --max-parallel bounds
the server threads that drive map children. Default 8, measured by
sweeping 4-46: 8 is consistently fastest (36-38s wall), unbounded (46)
is worst (~55s) from container engine contention.

The server understands required args when matching work to a runner.
Previously, there was no way to prevent a generic runner from running
something specific. We added this to force tests to run on a test runner
with its own pool. That wasn't enough and we still needed max-parallel
on run-then, but it allowed us to prevent the generic runner from trying
to run work that should be provided by pinned core results, so we kept
it. This prompted us to thread the required-pool arg through to
continuations automatically

FIX NESTED CONTAINERS ON ORBSTACK. Two issues: find ... | wc -l under
pipefail exits the script when the pack directory doesn't exist on a
fresh machine; and CAP_SYS_ADMIN alone doesn't let crun do pivot_root
inside a nested container — added --security-opt seccomp=unconfined.

Also: jless in the dev shell, /status times in milliseconds relative to
the root request, a /status walk can no longer abort the server,
chat-online reads its key from /secret, tests/std-lint becomes
tests/lint, deep-deps supports file dependencies, phase markers in
caos-tools/test, dev stack traces join the host's trace tree.

- Traces, when fetched over http, now show times in ms and relative to
  the request time of the root, to be more readable
- The number of trace lines shown by run and run-tool can be controlled
  with CAOS_WATCH_LINES